          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> Stacks, Queues and Lists are more or less what they sound like.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Firstly, a Stack is the simplest grouping of data and also the most important. Like in everyday life, a stack is a group of objects stacked into a pile and just as you wouldn’t pull the bottom book out of a stack of books, the top object in the stack or the last one added, is always first to be removed.</p>
<p>This principle is referred to as LIFO or Last in First Out.</p>
<p>So, the book or object in this case that was last added to the stack, would be the first to get removed.</p>
<p>A great example of a stack is the undo button or back button on a browser, when clicked, you want to go back to the last state or page, not the first.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> A Queue is similar to a stack in that it is a grouping of data but is different in the method that data is retrieved. It works the same as in real life for example when a queue is formed of people waiting for something, the last to join would not be the first to go. This is the same in coding, a principle is used which is referred to as FIFO or First in First Out. The object in this case that is first to enter the queue is the first to exit.</p>
<p>A great example of a Queue is a call waiting system at a call centre where you want the first people who dialled in, being the first callers that are handled by your team.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> A Linear List is a more memory intensive and a complicated version of a stack or queue. While a stack or queue is only ever as large as the objects stacked or queued together, a list is a prescribed amount of space. Just think about save slots for a game, usually there is a set amount, and if a player only ever saves their game in the same slot overwriting their previous save, all the memory allocated to those other slots is sat wasted. Conversely if a player keeps saving in a new slot each time, eventually they will run out of slots so will be unable to save any newer saves without replacing older ones.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
